MMPS Design Principles
1. No scripting required

A customer should never need to edit, understand, or even see LSL to use MMPS.

2. Discover, don't configure

Mission Control should find stations automatically.

The owner shouldn't have to tell the Console what exists.

3. Build like a venue owner thinks

Never ask:

Configure Device

Ask:

Set Up Stage Left Guitar

4. Every station is self-describing

A station should tell Mission Control:

who it is
what it can do
what services it provides
what capabilities those services support

Mission Control shouldn't have to guess.

5. One touch, one menu

A station has one owner.

One touch.

One menu.

Everything else is a service behind it.

That alone will make MMPS feel polished.

6. Consistency over cleverness

If a Guitar Station has:

Identity
Performer
Lighting
Props
Diagnostics

Then every station should present those same concepts whenever they apply.

Owners shouldn't have to relearn the interface.

7. Stations are independent

Deleting one station should never break another.

Adding ten new stations should require no Console update.

8. Mission Control coordinates

Stations perform.

Mission Control coordinates.

That distinction keeps responsibilities clear.

9. Builder tools stay hidden

A venue owner uses:

Mission Control

A builder uses:

Builder Mode

A performer uses:

Join Band

Each person sees only what they need.

10. Ship quality

And finally...

If this were a commercial product, would I be proud to ship this?

Every feature has to answer "yes."
